<BRK/>
<SCN>SECTION 32 12 19.16</SCN><BRK/>
<BRK/>
<STL>RESIN-MODIFIED ASPHALT PAVING WEARING COURSES</STL><BRK/>
<DTE>05/25</DTE><BRK/>
<NTE><BRK/>
<AST/><BRK/>
<NPR>NOTE:  This guide specification covers the requirements for <SCP>resin-modified asphalt 
paving wearing courses</SCP>.<BRK/>
<BRK/>
Resin-Modified Pavement material is generally<BRK/>
described as an open-graded asphalt concrete mixture containing 25- to 35-percent 
voids which are filled with a resin modified portland cement grout.  The open-graded 
asphalt mixture and resin modified cement grout are produced and placed separately.  
The open-graded mixture is produced in a typical asphalt concrete plant and 
placed with standard asphalt paving equipment.  After the open-graded layer 
has cooled, the grout is poured onto the porous surfacing and vibrated into 
the internal voids.  The RMP layer is typically 50 mm (2 in.) thick and has 
a surface appearance similar to a rough-textured PCC. The resulting pavement 
surfacing is fuel-resistant, rut-resistant, requires little to no maintenance, 
and potentially requires less cost and effort to construct when compared to 
PCC, but more than HMA.<BRK/>
<BRK/>
RMP is best suited for pavements that are subjected to low-speed traffic that 
is channelized or abrasive by nature.  Heavy equipment parking areas are the 
most frequent application. Pavement areas with heavy static point loads and 
heavy fuel spillage are also ideal RMP application candidates.  The practical 
limit for the surface slope of an RMP section is 2 percent.<BRK/>
<BRK/>
According to Waterways Experitment Station (WES, now ERDC) Report MP GL-96-7, 
the resin additive is a proprietary material produced in the United States by 
the Alyan Corporation of Virginia.  Internationally, the product is known as 
Salviacim, which is a registered trademark of Eurovia of France.  After the 
original publication of this specification, another similar product called Densiphalt 
is now available on the market.  Both products should use this specification.<BRK/>
<BRK/>
See TSPWG 3-260-02.01-8 (2019) for additional information and design criteria.<BRK/>
<BRK/>
Adhere to <URL HREF="https://www.wbdg.org/dod/ufc/ufc-1-300-02">UFC 1-300-02</URL> Unified Facilities Guide Specifications (UFGS) Format 
Standard when editing this guide specification or preparing new project specification 
sections.  Edit this guide specification for project specific requirements by 
adding, deleting, or revising text.  For bracketed items, choose applicable 
item(s) or insert appropriate information.<BRK/>
<BRK/>
Remove information and requirements not required in respective project, whether 
or not brackets are present.<BRK/>
<BRK/>
Comments, suggestions and recommended changes for this guide specification are 
welcome and should be submitted as a <URL HREF="https://www.wbdg.org/dod/ufgs">Criteria Change Request (CCR)</URL>.</NPR><BRK/>
<AST/><BRK/></NTE>
<BRK/>
<PRT><TTL>PART 1   GENERAL</TTL><BRK/>
<NTE><BRK/>
<AST/><BRK/>
<NPR>NOTE:  Consult a representative of the USACE Transportation Systems Mandatory 
Center of Expertise (TSC) in the planning and designing of a Resin Modified 
Pavement (RMP).</NPR><BRK/>
<AST/><BRK/></NTE>
<BRK/>
